WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.440
From a messy behind-the-scenes romance to
a Twitter tirade that sank the entire show,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.000
the making of Roseanne was just as chaotic
as the lives of its on-screen characters.

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:13.170
Getting Roseanne to the small screen wasn't
easy.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:18.020
In the 1980s, television producers Marcy Carsey
and Tom Werner wanted to make a series about

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.270
a working mother.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.550
They commissioned a script from writer Matt
Williams, and thought that Roseanne Barr's

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.359
"domestic goddess" persona would be a perfect
fit.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.020
But Barr and Williams were combative from
the start.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.500
Vanity Fair reported that Williams wanted
to call it Life and Stuff to keep Barr from

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.040
having too much control, but Barr wanted to
simply call it Roseanne.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.570
Barr got her title, but it was just the first
in a series of battles between the star and

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.140
the writer.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.055
"You know what would be really good for breakfast?"

00:00:44.055 --> 00:00:44.580
"What?"

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.580
"Pie."

00:00:46.214 --> 00:00:47.063
"Tell him no."

00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:47.563
"No."

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:53.840
While Williams is credited as the sole creator
of Roseanne, Barr reportedly wanted credit

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.290
since she felt the show was based on her life.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:01.000
Williams, Carsey, and Werner claimed it was
actually a matter with the Writers Guild of America.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.150
Mere weeks into production, Barr wanted Williams
fired and threatened to leave the show.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.000
Williams did bow out, but not before taking
a large financial settlement for his efforts.

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:14.909
There was never any doubt that John Goodman
would play Dan Conner on Roseanne.

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:19.250
Before being cast, Goodman had a series of
small roles in classic '80s films like Revenge

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.430
of the Nerds and Raising Arizona, but he was
hardly a star.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.329
Werner claimed that they were looking for
an actor who could match Barr's charisma,

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:29.689
and Goodman nailed his screen test.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:35.000
However, while ABC wanted Roseanne to move
quickly into production, Goodman had a scheduling conflict.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.450
But Werner and Casey were so confident of
his talents that they forfeited a coveted

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.189
time slot in order to keep Goodman in the
role.

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:45.939
"One kid down, two to go, Darlene."

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.149
In a career retrospective for Vanity Fair,
Goodman recalled how he liked the script and

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:52.130
immediately hit it off with Barr.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.060
Goodman left his audition knowing he had the
job, something that rarely happened to him.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.430
He found job security in the role of Dan Conner,
playing the character for years — not only

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:04.158
in Roseanne, but also its popular spin-off
series, The Conners.

00:02:04.158 --> 00:02:08.619
Barr and Tom Arnold were longtime friends
on the stand-up comedy circuit before they

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:14.450
fell in love while taping Roseanne's 1987
HBO comedy special, The Roseanne Barr Show.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:19.080
While Barr was married and Arnold was in a
relationship, they soon were inseparable.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.270
Arnold became, and was subsequently fired,
from his gig as Roseanne's warm-up comedian,

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.560
but he was eventually hired on the show's
writing staff after Barr's insistence.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.010
The pair married in 1990.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.750
In addition to playing the character Arnie
Thomas, Arnold ascended to executive producer

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.940
on Roseanne, and essentially took control
of Barr's career, leading Barr to fire most

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.940
of her team.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.700
At the time of their marriage, a source told
Vanity Fair,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.430
"Roseanne is oblivious to everything but Tom
Arnold."

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.470
The couple brought their outlandish behavior
to the set of Roseanne, even igniting rumors

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:55.903
that they were engaged in a three-way relationship
with their assistant-turned-executive Kim Silva.

00:02:55.903 --> 00:03:01.461
Soon after — in 1994 — Barr and Arnold's
marriage ended, and so did Arnold's time on Roseanne.

00:03:01.461 --> 00:03:07.034
Barr fired Arnold and Silva, and reportedly
changed the locks on their production office.

00:03:07.398 --> 00:03:11.129
Roseanne wasn't the only TV collaboration
between Barr and Arnold.

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:16.430
The pair produced multiple short-lived sitcoms,
including The Jackie Thomas Show and Tom.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.500
In 1992, while Roseanne was the number one
show on television, The Jackie Thomas Show

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.500
was designed to be a vehicle for Arnold, making
its debut that December.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.660
According to The New York Times, ABC was concerned
about putting the poorly tested show in the

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.689
coveted and highly lucrative time slot directly
after Roseanne.

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:37.439
The show had promising debut ratings, but
its decline in viewership left ABC hesitant

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:39.194
to renew it for another season.

00:03:39.194 --> 00:03:40.794
That's when things got ugly.

00:03:40.794 --> 00:03:41.590
"What happened?

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.220
Well, I'll tell you what happened, the big
monkey ate the little monkey."

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:51.189
In 1993, Barr reportedly threatened to take
Roseanne to another network in retaliation

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:55.599
for what she perceived to be disrespect over
The Jackie Thomas Show, all the while sending

00:03:55.599 --> 00:03:58.290
scathing faxes to negative critics.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.939
While Arnold told the press that Jackie Thomas
was picked up for a second season, it was

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:05.519
only a tactic to bully ABC into renewing the
show.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:09.860
Ultimately, Arnold left Jackie Thomas to develop
the sitcom Tom for CBS.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.820
But this new sitcom only had a short run in
1994 before being canceled.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:18.639
The Roseanne chicken shirt has become one
of television's most recognized pieces of

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:19.979
anti-fashion.

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:23.540
Barr wrote for Vulture that she clashed with
the wardrobe department early in the show's

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:27.410
production, so she ended up wearing her own
clothes on the set.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:31.720
After this dispute, Roseanne's wardrobe department
apparently developed a sense of humor.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:36.220
They introduced the chicken shirt — first
worn by Lecy Goranson's Becky — as a joke

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.380
in Season 2.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.070
But once Barr saw the design, its ugliness
became something of an artistic statement.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:46.979
The shirt went on to play heavily in Season
5's finale episode "Daughters and Other Strangers."

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:51.550
It rotated among cast members, with numerous
actors sporting the garment during the episode.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.770
The chicken shirt would live on as Roseanne's
apron design at her restaurant, the Lanford

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.110
Lunch Box.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.070
It also appears during Season 10's opening
credits.

00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:05.910
Called "incalculably ill-judged" by the Rotten
Tomatoes Critics Consensus, "strange, scattered,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:11.169
and self-conscious" by Vulture, and one of
the worst series finales of all-time by Variety,

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:16.039
Season 9 of Roseanne left longtime fans confused
and even alienated.

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:20.390
When Season 9 was announced, The New York
Times reported that both Barr and ABC decided

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.220
it would be the show's last.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:26.569
Breaking from the show's working-class family
strife, the final season saw the Conners win

00:05:26.569 --> 00:05:29.100
$108 million in the lottery.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.720
The season bounced between wish-fulfillment
stories and self-referential television parodies,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.380
with John Goodman's Dan Conner notably absent
from most of the season's episodes.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.539
This was due to Goodman walking away from
Roseanne because he was reportedly fed up

00:05:42.539 --> 00:05:44.259
with the on-set drama.

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:48.750
The Roseanne finale, when the entire lottery
premise is revealed to be Roseanne's fantasy

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.607
after Dan had passed away, remains controversial
to this day.

00:05:52.607 --> 00:05:54.276
"Yeah and I got you some notepads.

00:05:54.276 --> 00:05:56.246
"And I got you a dictionary and a thesaurus."

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.750
"You know, Stephen King started this way."

00:05:58.908 --> 00:06:03.580
Even though Season 9 was billed as the show's
swan song, there were talks of a Roseanne

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:04.819
spin-off series.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:09.340
The Los Angeles Times claimed the show would
have seen Roseanne and Michael Fishman's D.J.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.770
moving to Las Vegas.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.599
With ABC balking at the show's high price,
the idea didn't stick.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:20.069
An unlikely meeting of comedy divas happened
during Season 9's Halloween episode "Satan,

00:06:20.069 --> 00:06:25.319
Darling," when Roseanne crossed over with
the UK comedy sensation Absolutely Fabulous.

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:30.039
In the episode, newly minted millionaire Roseanne
and her sister Jackie attended a party in

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:34.830
New York City, where she meets London fashion
mavens Patsy and Edina — played by Joanna

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.610
Lumley and Jennifer Saunders, respectively.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:42.210
The foursome drink excessively and the episode
transitions into a parody of the 1968 horror

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.310
film Rosemary's Baby.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.361
Roseanne's connection to Absolutely Fabulous
is not entirely inorganic.

00:06:47.361 --> 00:06:52.490
In 1995, The New York Times reported that
Barr purchased the rights to produce an American

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.250
version of the BBC series.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:59.389
The project was a hot commodity in Hollywood,
but AbFab never got off the ground.

00:06:59.389 --> 00:07:04.120
Instead, Barr and Saunders collaborated on
"Satan, Darling," with Saunders later recounting,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.770
"It was very bizarre.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.160
We were quite confused.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.160
But it was fun."

00:07:08.256 --> 00:07:13.759
While Barr is revered as a trailblazing female
talent bucking the system of Hollywood, she's

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:16.400
also known for being a difficult boss.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.129
Barr admitted to ABC News that she made a
list of people whom she'd fire when the show

00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:24.050
reached number one in the ratings, and she
eventually followed through.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:27.960
Roseanne writer and executive producer Bruce
Helford was warned by his agent and lawyer

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.740
that Barr fires everyone after one year.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.330
Sure enough, he was eventually canned.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:37.160
Even future television powerhouse Chuck Lorre
was the focus of Barr's ire.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:42.099
He told the Television Academy that he was
almost fired because she hated his first script.

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:46.550
Writers on Roseanne recalled to EW how Barr
dehumanized them by referring to them by numbers

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:47.729
instead of names.

00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:51.900
Barr, on the other hand, claimed that the
practice stripped the writers of their self-entitlement

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.600
and that they learned something from the practice.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.180
While some writers clashed with her abrasive
management, Barr credits herself for championing

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:04.349
female writers and giving then-unknown talents
like Joss Whedon and Judd Apatow their first

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:05.349
writing gigs.

00:08:05.445 --> 00:08:10.789
Lecy Goranson and Sarah Chalke both played
the Conners' eldest daughter, Becky.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:14.080
Goranson originated the character, playing
her for the show's first five seasons.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.699
However, the role was recast when Goranson
decided to attend college.

00:08:17.699 --> 00:08:22.379
According to EW, after testing hundreds of
actors for the role, Chalke was cast.

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:26.520
While Chalke was around for part of Season
6 and all of Season 7, Goranson was able to

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.110
mix her higher education pursuits with her
sitcom stardom.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.849
She returned to Roseanne for parts of Season
8, sharing the role with Chalke.

00:08:33.849 --> 00:08:37.050
Though Chalke was the show's sole Becky for
Season 9.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.729
The humor of having two actors play the same
character — sometimes in the same season

00:08:40.729 --> 00:08:42.570
— was not lost on Roseanne.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:46.980
The show included self-aware jokes about Goranson
and Chalke playing the same role, including

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.510
a parody of The Patty Duke Show theme song
in Season 8.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:55.853
When Roseanne returned for Season 10 in 2018,
both Goranson and Chalke came back to the show.

00:08:55.853 --> 00:09:00.720
A new role was created for Chalke and, for
the first time, both actors shared the screen.

00:09:01.042 --> 00:09:05.730
Before Roseanne, Halloween-themed network
television episodes were few and far between.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.130
The show arguably contributed to the invention
of dedicated spooky season episodes.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:16.360
Roseanne Barr told Yahoo! Entertainment that
ABC initially resisted making Roseanne Halloween

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.130
episodes — fearing it would alienate part
of their audience.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:23.420
However, Barr argued that Halloween was popular,
and after she won the debate, she called the

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:27.500
victory, quote, "the first dragon we slayed
on the Roseanne show."

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:32.190
With the network greenlighting the concept,
the beloved tradition was born in Season 2's

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.785
episode "BOO!," where the Conners stage a
haunted house for trick-or-treaters.

00:09:35.785 --> 00:09:38.220
"This is the only way out.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.209
Follow me into the enchanted garage."

00:09:41.209 --> 00:09:45.600
In every subsequent season, the show pulled
out all the stops to celebrate Halloween.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.890
Elaborate costumes, hilarious pranks, and
frightful antics had viewers tuning in to

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.410
see who could out-scare whom.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.880
Goodman reportedly joked that the Conners
were poor because they spent all their money

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:57.160
on Halloween.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:01.690
Even if the Conners went broke celebrating
the season, the amount of fun they had was priceless.

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:05.590
Roseanne Barr wrote for Vulture that, when
developing the show, she needed Roseanne to

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:10.320
not just be funny, but also about the struggle
to survive, and breaking taboos.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.945
The show aired episodes on domestic violence,
racism, teen sex, and drugs; but not without resistance.

00:10:15.945 --> 00:10:20.550
Barr fought with network censors over several
issues, including episodes about menstruation

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.420
and same-sex kissing.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:26.800
The Season 1 episode "Nightmare on Oak Street"
made headlines for its groundbreaking depiction

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.560
of Sara Gilbert's Darlene experiencing her
first period.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.140
Barr later told Oprah that the episode met
with resistance from ABC, saying,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:37.180
"It was hard because that had never been on
television."

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.970
Later in the show's run, ABC alleged that
the Season 6 episode "Don't Ask, Don't Tell"

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:46.139
— which featured a kiss between Roseanne
and Mariel Hemingway's Sharon at a gay bar

00:10:46.139 --> 00:10:49.540
— violated network standards and refused
to air the episode.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:53.850
After Barr's demand that the episode be shown,
it was eventually released, reportedly garnering

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.560
nearly 20 million viewers.

00:10:55.837 --> 00:10:59.970
During its heyday, Roseanne was one of the
most popular television shows in America,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:04.629
so it might come as a surprise that it was
virtually snubbed by TV's most prestigious award.

00:11:04.742 --> 00:11:09.620
The show was a consistent hit for ABC and
was recognized by The New York Times in 1994

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.920
as "the most watched entertainment show in
television over the past five years."

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.740
Despite its popularity, Roseanne wasn't nominated
for a single Emmy Award for Outstanding Comedy

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.863
Series during its 10-season run.

00:11:20.863 --> 00:11:23.850
"Mom used to yell at us in the shower.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.680
It was like that scene in Psycho.

00:11:27.472 --> 00:11:29.571
Take out the trash!"

00:11:29.593 --> 00:11:33.820
Roseanne Barr lamented her initial lack of
Emmy recognition to the Los Angeles Times

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:37.649
and claimed it was likely retribution for
on-set controversies.

00:11:37.649 --> 00:11:42.389
Barr eventually won an Emmy for Outstanding
Lead Actress in a Comedy Series in 1993, with

00:11:42.389 --> 00:11:46.780
Goodman and Laurie Metcalf receiving multiple
Emmys for their work on the series.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:50.649
While Roseanne certainly earned its acting
accolades, it can be baffling that the show

00:11:50.649 --> 00:11:53.570
itself was never recognized in its totality.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:58.980
In 2017, the news broke that most of Roseanne's
original cast would reprise their roles for

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.340
a limited series revival on ABC.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:05.000
Even as the excitement mounted, there was
the problem of how to address the continuation

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.600
of the series after its Season 9 finale.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.647
Well, it's television after all, and Roseanne
chose to simply ignore most of Season 9's finale.

00:12:12.647 --> 00:12:17.620
In Season 10's opener, Dan is still alive
and other questions — like who exactly Darlene

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.829
and Becky married — were left unanswered.

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:24.300
The first episode of the Roseanne revival
pulled in over 18 million viewers, the highest

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.510
network comedy ratings in years.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:31.389
In fact, Roseanne was 2018's most-watched
series in total viewers.

00:12:31.389 --> 00:12:35.950
Despite its popularity, the celebration was
short-lived when ABC canceled the series after

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.620
Barr posted racist comments on Twitter.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.570
ABC Entertainment president Channing Dungey
stated,

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:45.940
"Roseanne's Twitter statement is abhorrent,
repugnant and inconsistent with our values,

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.980
and we have decided to cancel her show."

00:12:48.144 --> 00:12:53.370
Roseanne was a serious incubator for up-and-coming
Hollywood talent, with many members of the

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:56.300
show becoming some of entertainment's most
powerful players.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:01.060
Amy Sherman-Palladino — creator of Gilmore
Girls and The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel — super

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:05.480
producer Chuck Lorre, and Buffy the Vampire
Slayer mastermind Joss Whedon were all in

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:06.699
the writer's room.

00:13:06.699 --> 00:13:10.970
Judd Apatow shared on Tell Me More with Kelly
Corrigan that supporting Barr with her joke

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:15.779
writing was his first big job, and even Norm
Macdonald penned several Roseanne episodes

00:13:15.779 --> 00:13:18.560
before his achieving stardom on Saturday Night
Live.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.300
"You know, the old man was almost a state
champ back in high school."

00:13:21.865 --> 00:13:23.885
"Were you ever in a cage match?"

00:13:23.885 --> 00:13:25.800
"Only with your mom."

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:29.570
Although some might have forgotten George
Clooney on Roseanne, he played Jackie's love

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:32.600
interest Booker Brooks in the show's first
season.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.320
Before Clooney became an Oscar-winning movie
star and filmmaker, he was just a working

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.720
guy sporting a pencil behind his ear.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.730
While audiences became acquainted with Johnny
Galecki as David on Roseanne, he eventually

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:46.220
catapulted into global fame on The Big Bang
Theory.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:50.860
Sara Gilbert parlayed her role as Darlene
into a successful producing career, co-creating

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.980
the CBS chat show The Talk.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.689
She also initiated and executive produced
the Roseanne revival.

